# leakcheck config: the internal identifiers this PUBLIC repo must not publish.
#
# WHY IT IS ACCEPTABLE TO LIST THESE HERE
# A gate that looks for a name has to hold the name. Every entry below is a
# BARE TOKEN with no context: no owner, no address, no role, no location, no
# link to any account or customer. On its own a token here tells a reader
# nothing. That is the whole point, and it is why this one file is the single
# allowed exception to "no internal identifiers in a public repo". Keep it that
# way: never add a comment that explains what a token refers to, and never add
# an address, a hostname, a URL or a credential to this file. leakcheck skips
# this file when it scans, so anything else you put here ships unchecked.
#
# FORMAT
#   name: <token>     a machine name. Matched on word boundaries, case
#                     insensitive. A trailing "+" means "followed by digits",
#                     so "fttb+" covers fttb1, fttb2 and so on.
#   id:   <token>     an account identifier. Same matching rules.
#   exclude: <glob>   a path leakcheck must not scan. Use sparingly.
#
# IP address literals are NOT configured here. They are handled by an
# allow-list in scripts/leakcheck.py: only the ranges reserved for writing
# about (RFC 5737, RFC 3849, loopback, link-local, unspecified, broadcast,
# well-known multicast) pass. Everything else is ratcheted through the
# baseline.

name: ftb1
name: ftb2
name: fttb+

id: rjwrn
id: zfcbe

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PER-REPO EXCLUDES (clusteragent)
#
# Everything above this line is byte-identical to the other public repos.
# Everything below is data specific to this repo.
#
# Both entries below suppress a CREDENTIAL-class FALSE POSITIVE, not a leak.
# Credentials are never baselineable by design, and that design is right, so a
# false positive in that class has no ratchet to absorb it and would hold the
# release gate red for ever. An exclude is the only sanctioned lever the
# checker gives. It is a WORKAROUND, not the fix: the fix belongs in
# scripts/leakcheck.py and has to land in all four public repos at once.
# Delete both lines the moment that fix ships.
#
# Neither file contains any credential. Verified by reading both in full.

# Holds the Kubernetes Secret NAMES this agent reads, as plain constants:
#   ClusterAgentTLSSecret = "cluster-agent-tls"
# The credential rule's assignment heuristic sees the keyword "secret" (as the
# tail of the constant name) followed by = and a 16+ char quoted string. The
# sibling constant NodeAgentTLSSecret = "node-agent-tls" does NOT fire, purely
# because its value is 14 characters. A rule whose verdict turns on string
# length is matching a name, not a secret.
exclude: agentstream/k8s.go

# Tests the build-time credential plumbing, so it is credential-shaped by
# construction and always will be. Three false positives here:
#   - two /tmp fixture PATHS whose names end in "-token"
#   - a PEM header, "-----BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----", whose body is "abc"
# A test for code that reads ssh-privatekey Secrets has to feed it something
# PEM-shaped. This file carries no key material.
exclude: buildkitclient/buildauth_test.go
